# Keep the build context small and, more importantly, keep secrets out of it.
# Anything sent to the daemon can end up in an image layer.

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
secrets/

.git/
.github/

.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

frontend/node_modules/
frontend/.next/
frontend/out/

# Local databases and the Parquet lake. The live instance builds these on its
# volume; the demo regenerates from seed on boot.
data/
*.db
*.db-*
*.sqlite*
*.duckdb*
*.parquet
warehouse/target/
warehouse/dbt_packages/
warehouse/logs/

# tests/fixtures IS needed in the repo but not in the runtime image.
tests/
docs/
tasks/

# **Except the documents a shipped script OPENS at runtime.** The allowlist
# below is the same mechanism as `scripts/*`'s, one layer down, and it was
# found the same way: `dry_run_fair_price_downsample.py` was added to that
# allowlist, deployed, and crashed on the live box at the next line --
# `FileNotFoundError: /app/docs/measurements/2026-09-01-preregistration-
# fair-prices-downsample.md`. Shipping a program is not the same as shipping
# what the program reads, and the first guard reads exactly like the second.
#
# This file is a runtime dependency rather than documentation: the dry run
# reproduces numbered sections of its own registration verbatim in every run,
# so that an amendment reaches the output without anyone re-typing it. Cutting
# the document would silently strip §6 and §9 from the evidence -- the caveats
# that make the byte figure interpretable -- rather than fail loudly.
#
# `TestShippedScriptsCanReadTheFilesTheyOpen` derives this from the AST of
# every shipped script (`Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1] / ... ` chains),
# so the next one is caught in CI rather than on the box.
!docs/measurements/2026-09-01-preregistration-fair-prices-downsample.md

# Dev and capture tooling stays out. Everything here either hits the API or
# exists only to build fixtures -- EXCEPT the scripts `docker/entrypoint.sh`
# executes, which are part of the runtime and must survive into the image.
#
# This allowlist has now failed twice, the same way both times: the entrypoint
# gained a script and nobody added the matching `!` line. `run_loop.py` was the
# first -- an image that started, reported healthy and served pages while the
# one process that grows the evidence record was absent from the filesystem.
# `migrate_db.py` was the second, and it was louder only by luck: it runs under
# `set -e` before uvicorn, so the container exited 2 and crash-looped instead of
# serving a lie.
#
# `TestTheEntrypointRunsWhatItMustRunFirst` now derives this list from the
# script rather than trusting it to be maintained by hand.
#
# And it has now failed a THIRD time, in the half that derivation cannot reach.
# That test asks "which scripts does `entrypoint.sh` run?" -- so it covers the
# runtime, and only the runtime. `inspect_live_db.py` is never run by the
# entrypoint: it is invoked by hand over `flyctl ssh console`, under Joe's
# ruling that only a committed, reviewed script may be run by path. Nothing
# executes it at boot, so nothing derived it, so it was excluded from the image
# from the day it was written -- while four live questions were described as
# "riding on" it and a fee-round precondition was planned around deploying a new
# query into it.
#
# The failure mode is quieter than the first two. There is no crash loop and no
# silent absence of a process: the file simply is not there when someone ssh's
# in, months after the decision that depended on it.
#
# And it has now failed a FOURTH time, in that same underivable half. The
# volume lane added `scripts/dry_run_fair_price_downsample.py` -- the deciding
# instrument for a registered measurement, and by its own docstring "the only
# permitted source of the bytes figure" -- and never added the `!` line. It was
# absent from the image from the day it was written. What surfaced it was Joe
# handing over the documented `flyctl ssh console` command and getting
# `No such file or directory` back.
#
# **"Cannot be derived" was wrong, and that sentence is why this kept
# happening.** It read as a fact about the problem; it was a fact about where
# anyone had looked. Every ssh-invoked script already declares its own
# invocation in its docstring, naming the absolute path it runs at:
#
#     -C "python /app/scripts/dry_run_fair_price_downsample.py"
#
# So the marker was in all six of them the whole time.
# `TestTheSshInvokedScriptsSurviveDockerignore` derives this half from that
# self-reference, and the rule is self-reference rather than mention:
# `run_signal_test.py`, `analyze_prop_onesided.py` and
# `read_window_gate_observations.py` all contain `/app/scripts/` while naming
# `inspect_live_db.py`'s path, never their own. They are laptop-side and must
# stay out.
#
# And there is now a THIRD class, opened on 2026-09-06 and dangerous for a
# reason neither of the first two has: **a shipped script's own imports.**
#
# `inspect_live_db.py` reached 254,479 bytes -- 97% of the 262,144-byte
# ceiling at which the Read tool refuses a file outright, silently -- so it
# was split into an entrypoint plus seven domain modules
# (`scripts/inspect_live_db_*.py`). Only the entrypoint is invoked by path,
# so only the entrypoint declares `/app/scripts/inspect_live_db.py` in its
# docstring, so the ssh derivation above sees only the entrypoint. Ship that
# one file alone and the box gets an inspector that dies on its first import
# line -- and the way that surfaces is Joe at an ssh prompt during an
# incident, which is the moment this tooling exists for.
#
# The glob below is deliberately a PATTERN rather than seven names: a new
# domain module is covered the day it is written. And it is deliberately
# narrow -- `inspect_live_db_*` and not `inspect_*` -- so it can never widen
# into the laptop-only tools two lists down.
#
# `TestTheInspectorFamilySurvivesDockerignore` derives the requirement from
# the entrypoint's actual import statements, so the glob is checked rather
# than trusted, and a module imported under a name this pattern does not
# match turns red in CI rather than absent on the box.
#
# All three classes are now derived, and none is maintained by hand:
#   - scripts the entrypoint runs      (derived from `docker/entrypoint.sh`)
#   - scripts the ssh ruling invokes   (derived from each script's own docstring)
#   - modules a shipped script imports (derived from that script's imports)
# The `!` lines below remain the mechanism; the tests are what keep them honest.
scripts/*
!scripts/run_loop.py
!scripts/migrate_db.py
!scripts/inspect_live_db.py
!scripts/inspect_live_db_*.py
!scripts/inspect_live_disk.py
!scripts/inspect_live_proc.py
!scripts/fetch_live_route.py
!scripts/dry_run_fair_price_downsample.py
# Added 2026-09-07, and it is the FIFTH failure of this allowlist rather than a
# new script. `list_unmatched.py` was written for the laptop, and the live
# `unmatched_items` queue -- the only copy that has ever held real names -- had
# no instrument on the box at all. The NFL pre-flight read it as ad-hoc SQL over
# `flyctl ssh` instead, which the inspector's own ruling forbids. The script now
# declares `/app/scripts/list_unmatched.py`, so the derivation above demands
# this line; it is not kept by hand any more than the others are.
!scripts/list_unmatched.py

*.md
!README.md
